WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.010 --> 00:00:01.570
(peppy percussion music)

00:00:01.570 --> 00:00:03.870
- [Narrator] The fifth
generation of wireless technology

00:00:03.870 --> 00:00:06.400
has made it to cities
in the United States.

00:00:06.400 --> 00:00:08.860
Across the country,
T-Mobile, Sprint, and others

00:00:08.860 --> 00:00:12.180
are laying the foundation for
a faster, better internet,

00:00:12.180 --> 00:00:14.220
but in the race to develop 5G,

00:00:14.220 --> 00:00:16.770
U.S. firms are already at a disadvantage.

00:00:16.770 --> 00:00:19.440
They have limited access to
some of the radio frequencies

00:00:19.440 --> 00:00:21.970
that enable 5G, and operators worry

00:00:21.970 --> 00:00:25.240
that there's not enough space
to meet anticipated demand.

00:00:25.240 --> 00:00:27.420
In Chicago, Verizon download speeds

00:00:27.420 --> 00:00:30.770
reached a maximum above
one gigabyte per second.

00:00:30.770 --> 00:00:32.360
While maximums are interesting,

00:00:32.360 --> 00:00:34.680
median speeds say more about coverage.

00:00:34.680 --> 00:00:37.670
Look in South Korea, and you'll
see higher median speeds.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:40.973
That means their networks may
be faster in more scenarios.

00:00:41.820 --> 00:00:44.530
To understand why the
U.S. has a disadvantage,

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:47.050
you've got to understand
how wireless technology,

00:00:47.050 --> 00:00:49.580
like 5G, works.
(pleasant electronic music)

00:00:49.580 --> 00:00:52.410
Most wireless systems rely on radio waves

00:00:52.410 --> 00:00:54.360
that can carry signals back and forth

00:00:54.360 --> 00:00:57.150
between your device
and a service provider.

00:00:57.150 --> 00:01:00.410
To run these systems, firms
like Sprint or Vodafone

00:01:00.410 --> 00:01:03.820
have to buy space in the
radio frequency spectrum.

00:01:03.820 --> 00:01:05.460
The left side of the spectrum chart

00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:07.700
represents low frequencies.

00:01:07.700 --> 00:01:10.910
Radio waves in this portion
of spectrum are long

00:01:10.910 --> 00:01:13.615
and can travel farther distances.

00:01:13.615 --> 00:01:16.040
Think of it like playing
heavy bass in a building.

00:01:16.040 --> 00:01:18.700
The low frequencies can
be heard from a distance,

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:22.160
even if they travel through
solid objects like walls.

00:01:22.160 --> 00:01:24.630
Frequencies toward the
middle left of this chart

00:01:24.630 --> 00:01:26.180
are in the mid band.

00:01:26.180 --> 00:01:29.080
These frequencies, between
one and six gigahertz,

00:01:29.080 --> 00:01:30.370
are quite useful.

00:01:30.370 --> 00:01:33.140
They're low enough to send
signals a long distance,

00:01:33.140 --> 00:01:34.640
but they have an advantage.

00:01:34.640 --> 00:01:37.470
They can carry more data than low bands.

00:01:37.470 --> 00:01:42.010
Some versions of 4G and WiFi
live in this sweet spot band.

00:01:42.010 --> 00:01:45.420
The main issue here is
that space is limited.

00:01:45.420 --> 00:01:48.880
Move to the right, and the
frequencies get higher still.

00:01:48.880 --> 00:01:52.120
As frequencies get higher,
their wavelengths shrink.

00:01:52.120 --> 00:01:55.000
Shorter wavelengths let
engineers cram more data

00:01:55.000 --> 00:01:56.920
into their radio signal.

00:01:56.920 --> 00:02:00.230
Firms in the United States are
mostly building 5G networks

00:02:00.230 --> 00:02:03.000
in this higher frequency
portion of spectrum.

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:05.690
The ultra-fast download
speeds and low latency

00:02:05.690 --> 00:02:08.320
will enable developments
in self-driving cars

00:02:08.320 --> 00:02:12.000
and virtual reality, but
the short wavelengths here

00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:14.020
present technical issues.
(pleasant orchestral music)

00:02:14.020 --> 00:02:17.300
For starters, the waves
don't travel very far.

00:02:17.300 --> 00:02:20.180
This means that your signal
might not work indoors

00:02:20.180 --> 00:02:22.100
or if anything blocks your line of sight

00:02:22.100 --> 00:02:23.770
to the tower, like a tree.

00:02:23.770 --> 00:02:26.160
The inability to travel great distances

00:02:26.160 --> 00:02:29.490
also means that cities will
need to install several nodes

00:02:29.490 --> 00:02:31.600
to ensure a blanket of coverage.

00:02:31.600 --> 00:02:34.353
The buildout of that
infrastructure will not be cheap.

00:02:35.580 --> 00:02:38.800
So why are U.S. firms at a disadvantage?

00:02:38.800 --> 00:02:41.100
It has to do with governance.

00:02:41.100 --> 00:02:43.160
The International Telecommunication Union

00:02:43.160 --> 00:02:45.620
divides the world into three regions.

00:02:45.620 --> 00:02:46.690
Within the regions,

00:02:46.690 --> 00:02:49.500
countries negotiate to
reserve frequencies.

00:02:49.500 --> 00:02:51.700
Operators then bid for a portion.

00:02:51.700 --> 00:02:54.780
Think of it like an auction
for invisible real estate.

00:02:54.780 --> 00:02:56.110
In the United States,

00:02:56.110 --> 00:02:58.650
two groups control that bidding process.

00:02:58.650 --> 00:03:01.540
The FCC holds auctions for private groups

00:03:01.540 --> 00:03:05.580
like Sprint and T-Mobile,
and the NTIA does the same

00:03:05.580 --> 00:03:08.300
for federal agencies like
the Defense Department.

00:03:08.300 --> 00:03:10.180
- The Department of Defense
has traditionally been

00:03:10.180 --> 00:03:12.280
one of the biggest
spectrum users in the U.S.,

00:03:12.280 --> 00:03:14.773
and so they are the
incumbent in many bands,

00:03:15.810 --> 00:03:18.150
and any bands where
you could move them out

00:03:18.150 --> 00:03:20.320
has been done long ago.

00:03:20.320 --> 00:03:22.820
- [Narrator] As a result,
U.S. firms have less space

00:03:22.820 --> 00:03:25.690
to build 5G networks that
balance higher data speeds

00:03:25.690 --> 00:03:27.480
with reasonable coverage.

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:29.770
Countries in other
regions have more access

00:03:29.770 --> 00:03:31.640
to that spectrum sweet spot,

00:03:31.640 --> 00:03:34.090
so China, Japan, and South Korea

00:03:34.090 --> 00:03:36.993
are building out midband
and high band all at once.

00:03:38.140 --> 00:03:41.130
For its part, the FCC wants
to help domestic firms

00:03:41.130 --> 00:03:43.180
roll out 5G fast.

00:03:43.180 --> 00:03:45.800
- My plan includes three key components,

00:03:45.800 --> 00:03:49.100
freeing up spectrum, promoting
wireless infrastructure,

00:03:49.100 --> 00:03:51.470
and modernizing regulations.

00:03:51.470 --> 00:03:55.210
The FCC is moving forward
aggressively on all fronts.

00:03:55.210 --> 00:03:57.850
- [Narrator] The three
5G-related auctions in 2019

00:03:57.850 --> 00:04:00.010
are for those faster high bands,

00:04:00.010 --> 00:04:02.280
which means that early
rollouts in the United States

00:04:02.280 --> 00:04:04.610
will mostly be that fast download,

00:04:04.610 --> 00:04:07.103
limited coverage flavor of 5G.

00:04:08.040 --> 00:04:10.930
Firms in other countries
don't have that limitation.

00:04:10.930 --> 00:04:13.330
Because they have more
access to the mid band,

00:04:13.330 --> 00:04:15.910
their 5G networks will offer wide coverage

00:04:15.910 --> 00:04:18.190
alongside blazing fast speeds.

00:04:18.190 --> 00:04:21.200
Many analysts anticipate
that companies in China

00:04:21.200 --> 00:04:24.370
will lead the charge
toward a mid band-type 5G.

00:04:24.370 --> 00:04:26.800
And leading the global
market early is important

00:04:26.800 --> 00:04:28.970
according to the Defense Innovation Board.

00:04:28.970 --> 00:04:30.940
Previous market leaders in wireless tech

00:04:30.940 --> 00:04:33.740
set standards that the rest
of the world had to follow.

00:04:33.740 --> 00:04:35.620
That's good for business.

00:04:35.620 --> 00:04:38.400
Analysts say that U.S.
firms have some flexibility

00:04:38.400 --> 00:04:41.910
to repurpose their
existing mid band spectrum.

00:04:41.910 --> 00:04:43.710
AT&amp;T, for example, will introduce

00:04:43.710 --> 00:04:47.230
their mid band Sub-6 network in 2020.

00:04:47.230 --> 00:04:50.150
As the decade advances,
firms in the United States

00:04:50.150 --> 00:04:53.350
are expected to lobby for more
access across the spectrum,

00:04:53.350 --> 00:04:55.810
but those negotiations will take time.

00:04:55.810 --> 00:04:58.230
One FCC commissioner
said that in the past,

00:04:58.230 --> 00:05:01.210
these agreements have
taken five to 10 years,

00:05:01.210 --> 00:05:03.080
which means that for the near-term,

00:05:03.080 --> 00:05:07.004
5G in the U.S. will be fast
but not very accessible.

00:05:07.004 --> 00:05:10.671
(pleasant synth rock music)

